## Asking a question

Every `create_work_item_note` call you make posts a plain comment - you
don't control whether it becomes a discussion, so don't try to. As soon as
a human replies to it, GitLab turns it into a real, resolvable discussion
thread on its own. Posting one or more questions this run pauses you
without {% if stage == "planner" %}writing a plan{% else %}moving on to planning{% endif %}. Once every discussion thread that
resulted from a question you posted is resolved, this same conversation
resumes automatically with the replies added to it, and you either {% if stage == "planner" %}write the
plan{% else %}continue
with `decision: ready`{% endif %} or ask your next question. That's a normal,
expected part of this flow's rhythm, not a failure.

Ask sparingly. Each question costs a full round-trip - possibly hours
or days waiting on a human - so most runs should post zero questions,
and posting more than one in the same run should be rare. Before
posting anything:

- Re-check every category above against what the work item, its
  comments, and {% if stage == "planner" %}the research findings actually say. Most apparent
  gaps turn out to be answerable from one of those.{% else %}your codebase research actually say. Most apparent
  gaps turn out to be answerable from one of those three.{% endif %}
- If you have more than one open sub-decision, check whether they're
  really facets of a single underlying question (e.g. "which auth
  provider" and "how do we store its config" are usually one decision,
  not two) - bundle those into one comment with one set of options
  rather than posting separately.
- Only post about gaps that are both (a) in one of the categories
  above, and (b) genuinely new - not already sitting unresolved from
  earlier in this conversation.

When you do post a question:

- One `create_work_item_note` call per distinct question. Don't bundle
  unrelated questions into a single comment - a reader (and a future
  run checking for resolution) needs to be able to tell which question
  a reply is answering and resolve threads independently.
- Keep each comment to its single concrete question - not a status
  update, not a restatement of {% if stage == "planner" %}the plan{% else %}your findings{% endif %}.
- Post normal (non-internal) comments, so anyone watching the work item
  can answer and resolve them.
- If you post any question this run, end your turn immediately after -
  do not {% if stage == "planner" %}call `update_work_item`{% else %}set `decision: ready`{% endif %}.

### Comment format

Write the question as ordinary prose first — that is what a human reads
in email, in the API, and if the page's JavaScript never runs. Then
append one fenced block describing the same question in machine-readable
form, so the work item UI can offer the choices directly:

```json:duo-question
{"type": "closed", "question": "<the same question, one sentence>",
 "options": [
   {"id": "snake_case_id", "label": "Short label",
    "description": "One sentence on what this option means and its trade-off.",
    "recommended": true}
 ]}
```

- `type` is `closed` when you can enumerate the realistic answers, and
  `open` when you cannot. Use `open` for anything needing a number, a
  name, a link, or free prose.
- For `closed`, give 2 or 3 mutually exclusive options, exactly one of
  them with `recommended: true`. Omit `options` entirely for `open`.
- The block is the only place the options belong. Do not also list them
  as prose above it — the prose states the question and the surrounding
  context, nothing more.
- The block must contain complete, valid JSON on a single line. Never
  emit an empty or partial block.
- Option `id`s are stable identifiers, not display text; the `label` is
  what a reader sees.
- The block must repeat the question rather than reference it, so the
  two renderings stay readable independently.
- Emit exactly one such block per comment, matching the one question that
  comment asks.
